begin model

#@title: Mitochondrial MT1 cAMP signaling, fitting form (Suofu et al., 2017)

#@description: |
#  Fitting form of the compartmental cAMP model of Suofu et al. (2017): the
#  library model
#  models/mitochondrial_mt1_camp_signaling_suofu2017/
#  mitochondrial_mt1_camp_signaling_suofu2017.bngl with its simulation actions
#  removed, because PyBNF synthesizes the basal pre-equilibration and the two
#  stimulations from the conf's condition: records. The two agonist switches
#  MT1_isActive and OR_isActive are what those records set; the twelve rate
#  constants of Supplementary Data Table 1 plus the PM/OMM MT1 concentration
#  ratio are the free parameters. Nominals are this job's best fit.
#
#  Compartmental model of Gi-coupled receptor control of cAMP in a neuron that
#  carries the melatonin type-1 receptor (MT1) on BOTH the plasma membrane (PM)
#  and the outer mitochondrial membrane (OMM). Forskolin-activated adenylyl
#  cyclase (AC) on each membrane converts adenine nucleotide in the adjacent
#  aqueous compartment -- cytosol (CY) or mitochondrial lumen (ML) -- to cAMP;
#  phosphodiesterase (PDE) converts cAMP to AMP and AMP is recycled to ATP, so
#  each compartment runs a closed ATP -> cAMP -> AMP -> ATP cycle whose
#  steady state is set by AC and PDE activity. Nucleotides, and only
#  nucleotides, cross the OMM. Agonist-occupied receptor catalytically converts
#  Gi to Gi*, and Gi* sequesters AC in an inactive AC.Gi* complex that cannot
#  bind ATP. All steps are mass action; the network is finite (26 species), so
#  this is a deterministic ODE model.
#
#  Two stimulations are simulated from the same equilibrated basal state.
#  Melatonin switches on MT1 at the PM *and* at the OMM (the paper's "model 2");
#  DAMGO switches on the mu-opioid receptor, which is present only at the PM
#  ("model 1"). The behavior of interest is a reversal of compartmental
#  efficacy. Under melatonin, luminal cAMP falls further and faster than
#  cytosolic cAMP (to ~0.2 vs ~0.45 of basal at 600 s) because the OMM carries
#  the full receptor density while the PM carries only a fraction of it. Under
#  DAMGO the order reverses (PM ~0.03, ML ~0.30): the mitochondrion has no
#  receptor, and its cAMP falls only as far as diffusive exchange with the
#  emptying cytosol drags it, while ongoing luminal AC activity holds it up.
#  Because the cytosol is ~88x the luminal volume, that exchange is one-sided:
#  it props the mitochondrion up under melatonin and drains it under DAMGO.

#@keyword: |
#  signaling, G protein-coupled receptor, melatonin, MT1 receptor, mu-opioid
#  receptor, mitochondria, outer mitochondrial membrane, cyclic AMP, adenylyl
#  cyclase, phosphodiesterase, Gi, compartmental model, mass action,
#  monostable, Mus musculus

#@reference: |
#  Suofu Y, Li W, Jean-Alphonse FG, Jia J, Khattar NK, Li J, et al. (2017).
#  Dual role of mitochondria in producing melatonin and driving GPCR signaling
#  to block cytochrome c release. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 114:E7997-E8006.
#  doi:10.1073/pnas.1705768114 -- SI Appendix "Model constructions and
#  analysis", Supplementary Data Table 1 (reactions), Supplementary Data
#  Table 2 (initial quantities), Supplementary Data Fig. S7 (posteriors),
#  Fig. 4H/4I (data) and Fig. 4J (simulated curves reproduced here).

#@note: |
#  PROVENANCE OF THE PARAMETER VALUES. Suofu et al. (2017) list the eight
#  reaction types and name their twelve rate constants (Supplementary Data
#  Table 1) and give the initial quantities (Supplementary Data Table 2), but
#  they report the fitted rate constants only as replica-exchange MCMC
#  posterior histograms on a log10 axis (Supplementary Data Fig. S7) whose unit
#  convention is not stated. The values below are therefore NOT transcribed
#  from the paper: they are a redetermination against the paper's own fit
#  targets -- the averaged cAMP time courses of Fig. 4H (melatonin) and Fig. 4I
#  (DAMGO) -- carried out with PyBioNetFit in
#  pybnf-jobs/Suofu-2017/mito_camp/, which also documents the digitization.
#  Everything else (geometry, species, reactions, initial quantities) is the
#  paper's.
#
#  COMPARTMENTS AND UNITS. Compartment sizes are physical: volumes in um^3 per
#  cell and areas in um^2 per cell, from the paper's geometry (a 1 pL spherical
#  cell holding 100 spherical mitochondria of radius 0.3 um). Species amounts
#  are molecule counts, so BioNetGen's compartmental scaling divides every
#  bimolecular rate by the volume of the adjacent aqueous compartment (V_cy for
#  a PM/CY reaction, V_ml for an OMM/ML reaction) and second-order rate
#  constants carry units of um^3 per molecule per second. That scaling is what
#  makes the paper's construction work: luminal amounts are cytosolic amounts
#  times V_ml/V_cy, so the two compartments have identical concentrations and
#  identical intrinsic kinetics, and every difference between them comes from
#  receptor density and from diffusive exchange.
#
#  NUCLEOTIDES AS ONE MOLECULE TYPE. ATP, cAMP and AMP are the three states of
#  a single molecule type Nuc, which keeps the adenine-nucleotide pool
#  conserved by construction and lets one transport rule move all three species
#  across the OMM. The paper lists them as separate species; the generated
#  networks are equivalent.
#
#  SPECIES COUNT. Network generation gives 26 species where Suofu et al. (2017)
#  report 30 ODEs. The four not represented here are the mu-opioid receptor and
#  its Gi complex on the OMM, which the paper's model carries at zero and which
#  the compartment-explicit rules below never create, and the two agonists,
#  which are applied here as a switch on receptor activity rather than as
#  bound ligand (the paper likewise adds "active copies" of receptor rather
#  than simulating ligand binding).
#
#  COMPETITIVE INHIBITION OF AC. Rule 1 requires the Gi* site of AC to be free
#  and rule 8 requires its substrate site to be free, so AC.ATP.Gi* is never
#  formed. This reproduces Supplementary Data Table 1, which lists AC.ATP and
#  AC.Gi* as the only AC complexes.

begin parameters

  # === Physical constants and unit-conversion parameters (house style 1.3) ===
  # Avogadro constant
  NA 6.02214076e23 # molecules/mol
  PI 3.141592653589793 # dimensionless
  # Effective membrane-adjacent layer used to map surface densities to volumes
  h_mem 0.01 # um
  # Subvolume scaling factor: one whole cell is simulated
  f 1 # dimensionless

  # === Cell geometry; see SI Appendix "Model constructions and analysis" in
  # Suofu et al. (2017): a spherical 1 pL cell holding 100 spherical
  # mitochondria of radius 0.3 um. Parameters are ordered so that no expression
  # refers forward; run_network resolves the parameters block in file order. ===
  V_cy f*1000 # um^3/cell
  R_cell (3*V_cy/(4*PI))^(1/3) # um
  A_pm 4*PI*R_cell^2 # um^2/cell
  n_mito f*100 # mitochondria/cell
  r_mito 0.3 # um
  V_ml n_mito*(4/3)*PI*r_mito^3 # um^3/cell
  A_omm n_mito*4*PI*r_mito^2 # um^2/cell
  # Luminal fraction of the cell volume; every luminal amount is phi x cytosolic
  phi V_ml/V_cy # dimensionless
  # Extracellular fluid; no species live here, but BioNetGen needs a 3D root
  V_ec f*1e5 # um^3/cell
  # Reference volume: the whole cell, modeled as a 1 pL sphere
  V_ref 1e-15*V_cy # L/cell

  # === Initial quantities; Supplementary Data Table 2 in Suofu et al. (2017) ===
  Gi_CY_0 f*3.0e6 # molecules
  AC_CY_0 f*3.0e5 # molecules
  PDE_CY_0 f*2.4e5 # molecules
  ATP_CY_0 f*1.0e9 # molecules
  cAMP_CY_0 f*1.0e7 # molecules
  Gi_ML_0 Gi_CY_0*phi # molecules
  AC_ML_0 AC_CY_0*phi # molecules
  PDE_ML_0 PDE_CY_0*phi # molecules
  ATP_ML_0 ATP_CY_0*phi # molecules
  cAMP_ML_0 cAMP_CY_0*phi # molecules

  # Receptor densities. mu-OR sits at 1e6 copies on the PM (2068/um^2) and is
  # absent from the OMM; the PM/OMM MT1 concentration ratio ratio_MT1 <= 1 is
  # the paper's one free non-kinetic parameter. How much MT1 the OMM carries is
  # reported two ways that disagree by A_omm/(A_pm*phi) = 20.7-fold:
  # Supplementary Data Table 2 lists 1.13e4 copies, i.e. 1e6*phi (same
  # CONCENTRATION as a PM loaded with 1e6), while the SI Appendix text specifies
  # "2068 MT1 per square um of OMM", i.e. the same AREAL DENSITY as that PM,
  # which is 2.34e5 copies. MT1_OMM_amp selects between them: 1 is Table 2,
  # 20.68 is the text, and it is a free parameter of this job.
  R_ref f*1.0e6 # molecules
  ratio_MT1 0.0822213 # dimensionless
  MT1_OMM_amp 1.13002 # dimensionless
  MT1_OMM_0 R_ref*phi*MT1_OMM_amp # molecules
  MT1_PM_0 R_ref*ratio_MT1 # molecules
  OR_PM_0 R_ref # molecules

  # === Agonist switches (0 = silent receptor, 1 = saturating agonist) ===
  # A saturating agonist is modeled, as in Suofu et al. (2017), by turning on
  # the receptor's ability to load Gi rather than by simulating ligand binding.
  MT1_isActive 0 # dimensionless
  OR_isActive 0 # dimensionless

  # === Rate constants; Supplementary Data Table 1 in Suofu et al. (2017),
  # redetermined in pybnf-jobs/Suofu-2017/mito_camp (see the note above) ===
  # 1. ATP binding to adenylyl cyclase
  k1f 1.14394e-05 # um^3/molecule/s
  k1r 0.00304797 # /s
  # 2. cAMP production
  k2 0.25137 # /s
  # 3. cAMP binding to phosphodiesterase
  k3f 4.45701e-05 # um^3/molecule/s
  k3r 0.00183852 # /s
  # 4. cAMP hydrolysis to AMP
  k4 3.30566 # /s
  # 5. AMP recycling to ATP
  k5 32.2976 # /s
  # 6. Gi loading onto an agonist-occupied receptor
  k6 1.12318e-06 # um^3/molecule/s
  # 7. Gi activation and release
  k7 4147.7 # /s
  # 8. Sequestration of adenylyl cyclase by active Gi
  k8f 0.029857 # um^3/molecule/s
  k8r 0.000220863 # /s
  # Nucleotide permeation of the OMM, written as the efflux (ML -> CY) rate
  # constant; the influx constant is phi times smaller so that the two
  # compartments equilibrate at equal concentration, not at equal count.
  kdiff 0.00963586 # /s
  kdiff_out kdiff # /s
  kdiff_in kdiff*phi # /s

  # Gated Gi-loading constants, one per receptor population
  k6_MT1_PM k6*MT1_isActive # um^3/molecule/s
  k6_MT1_OMM k6*MT1_isActive # um^3/molecule/s
  k6_OR_PM k6*OR_isActive # um^3/molecule/s


end parameters

begin compartments

  # Extracellular fluid (empty; present only so that PM has a 3D parent)
  EC 3 V_ec
  # Plasma membrane
  PM 2 A_pm EC
  # Cytosol
  CY 3 V_cy PM
  # Outer mitochondrial membrane, pooled over the 100 mitochondria
  OMM 2 A_omm CY
  # Mitochondrial lumen, pooled over the 100 mitochondria
  ML 3 V_ml OMM

end compartments

begin molecule types

  # Adenylyl cyclase; sub = substrate (ATP) site, gi = active-Gi site
  AC(sub,gi)
  # Heterotrimeric Gi alpha subunit; r = receptor site, ac = cyclase site,
  # act = nucleotide-exchange state (0 = Gi, 1 = Gi*)
  Gi(r,ac,act~0~1)
  # Melatonin type-1 receptor; g = G protein site
  MT1(g)
  # Adenine nucleotide, one molecule type in three interconverting states
  Nuc(b,s~ATP~cAMP~AMP)
  # mu-opioid receptor; g = G protein site
  OR(g)
  # Phosphodiesterase; sub = substrate (cAMP) site
  PDE(sub)

end molecule types

begin seed species

  # Cytosol / plasma membrane
  Nuc(b,s~ATP)@CY ATP_CY_0 # molecules
  Nuc(b,s~cAMP)@CY cAMP_CY_0 # molecules
  Nuc(b,s~AMP)@CY 0 # molecules
  Gi(r,ac,act~0)@CY Gi_CY_0 # molecules
  PDE(sub)@CY PDE_CY_0 # molecules
  AC(sub,gi)@PM AC_CY_0 # molecules
  MT1(g)@PM MT1_PM_0 # molecules
  OR(g)@PM OR_PM_0 # molecules

  # Mitochondrial lumen / outer mitochondrial membrane
  Nuc(b,s~ATP)@ML ATP_ML_0 # molecules
  Nuc(b,s~cAMP)@ML cAMP_ML_0 # molecules
  Nuc(b,s~AMP)@ML 0 # molecules
  Gi(r,ac,act~0)@ML Gi_ML_0 # molecules
  PDE(sub)@ML PDE_ML_0 # molecules
  AC(sub,gi)@OMM AC_ML_0 # molecules
  MT1(g)@OMM MT1_OMM_0 # molecules

end seed species

begin observables

  # Free cAMP -- what the compartment-targeted FRET sensors report and what
  # Fig. 4J of Suofu et al. (2017) plots
  Molecules cAMP_CY Nuc(b,s~cAMP)@CY
  Molecules cAMP_ML Nuc(b,s~cAMP)@ML
  # Remaining nucleotide pools
  Molecules ATP_CY Nuc(b,s~ATP)@CY
  Molecules ATP_ML Nuc(b,s~ATP)@ML
  Molecules AMP_CY Nuc(b,s~AMP)@CY
  Molecules AMP_ML Nuc(b,s~AMP)@ML
  # Active G protein and the fraction of cyclase it has sequestered
  Molecules GiA_CY Gi(act~1)@CY
  Molecules GiA_ML Gi(act~1)@ML
  Molecules ACfree_PM AC(sub,gi)@PM
  Molecules ACfree_OMM AC(sub,gi)@OMM
  Molecules ACGi_PM AC(gi!1)@PM.Gi(ac!1)@CY
  Molecules ACGi_OMM AC(gi!1)@OMM.Gi(ac!1)@ML
  # Conserved totals (constant; useful as an integration check)
  Molecules Nuc_CY Nuc(b!?)@CY
  Molecules Nuc_ML Nuc(b!?)@ML

end observables

begin functions

  # cAMP as a percentage of the Supplementary Data Table 2 basal quantity.
  # These are the fit observables: the .exp columns are the Fig. 4H/4I traces,
  # which are percentages of the pre-agonist maximum, so fitting them against
  # this ratio also requires the pre-agonist steady state to sit at the
  # Table 2 quantity.
  camp_CY_pct() 100*cAMP_CY/cAMP_CY_0
  camp_ML_pct() 100*cAMP_ML/cAMP_ML_0

  # Dimensional conversions (uncomment for concentration output)
# cAMP_CY_conc() cAMP_CY/(NA*1e-15*V_cy) # M
# cAMP_ML_conc() cAMP_ML/(NA*1e-15*V_ml) # M
# ACfree_PM_dens() ACfree_PM/A_pm # molecules/um^2
# ACfree_OMM_dens() ACfree_OMM/A_omm # molecules/um^2

end functions

begin reaction rules

  # --- Cytosolic ATP/cAMP/AMP cycle, driven by adenylyl cyclase on the PM ---
  # 1. ATP binds the substrate site of a cyclase that is free of active Gi
  AC(sub,gi)@PM+Nuc(b,s~ATP)@CY<->AC(sub!1,gi)@PM.Nuc(b!1,s~ATP)@CY k1f,k1r
  # 2. Cyclization: bound ATP leaves as cAMP
  AC(sub!1,gi)@PM.Nuc(b!1,s~ATP)@CY->AC(sub,gi)@PM+Nuc(b,s~cAMP)@CY k2
  # 3. cAMP binds phosphodiesterase
  PDE(sub)@CY+Nuc(b,s~cAMP)@CY<->PDE(sub!1)@CY.Nuc(b!1,s~cAMP)@CY k3f,k3r
  # 4. Hydrolysis: bound cAMP leaves as AMP
  PDE(sub!1)@CY.Nuc(b!1,s~cAMP)@CY->PDE(sub)@CY+Nuc(b,s~AMP)@CY k4
  # 5. AMP is recycled to ATP
  Nuc(b,s~AMP)@CY->Nuc(b,s~ATP)@CY k5

  # --- Luminal ATP/cAMP/AMP cycle, driven by adenylyl cyclase on the OMM ---
  AC(sub,gi)@OMM+Nuc(b,s~ATP)@ML<->AC(sub!1,gi)@OMM.Nuc(b!1,s~ATP)@ML k1f,k1r
  AC(sub!1,gi)@OMM.Nuc(b!1,s~ATP)@ML->AC(sub,gi)@OMM+Nuc(b,s~cAMP)@ML k2
  PDE(sub)@ML+Nuc(b,s~cAMP)@ML<->PDE(sub!1)@ML.Nuc(b!1,s~cAMP)@ML k3f,k3r
  PDE(sub!1)@ML.Nuc(b!1,s~cAMP)@ML->PDE(sub)@ML+Nuc(b,s~AMP)@ML k4
  Nuc(b,s~AMP)@ML->Nuc(b,s~ATP)@ML k5

  # --- Nucleotide permeation of the outer mitochondrial membrane ---
  # Only free nucleotide crosses; proteins stay in their compartments
  Nuc(b)@CY<->Nuc(b)@ML kdiff_in,kdiff_out

  # --- Receptor/G protein coupling and G protein activation (rules 6 and 7) ---
  # MT1 on the plasma membrane loads cytosolic Gi
  MT1(g)@PM+Gi(r,ac,act~0)@CY->MT1(g!1)@PM.Gi(r!1,ac,act~0)@CY k6_MT1_PM
  MT1(g!1)@PM.Gi(r!1,ac,act~0)@CY->MT1(g)@PM+Gi(r,ac,act~1)@CY k7
  # MT1 on the outer mitochondrial membrane loads luminal Gi
  MT1(g)@OMM+Gi(r,ac,act~0)@ML->MT1(g!1)@OMM.Gi(r!1,ac,act~0)@ML k6_MT1_OMM
  MT1(g!1)@OMM.Gi(r!1,ac,act~0)@ML->MT1(g)@OMM+Gi(r,ac,act~1)@ML k7
  # mu-OR is confined to the plasma membrane
  OR(g)@PM+Gi(r,ac,act~0)@CY->OR(g!1)@PM.Gi(r!1,ac,act~0)@CY k6_OR_PM
  OR(g!1)@PM.Gi(r!1,ac,act~0)@CY->OR(g)@PM+Gi(r,ac,act~1)@CY k7

  # --- Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase by active Gi (rule 8) ---
  # Requires a free substrate site, so AC.ATP.Gi* is never formed
  AC(sub,gi)@PM+Gi(r,ac,act~1)@CY<->AC(sub,gi!1)@PM.Gi(r,ac!1,act~1)@CY k8f,k8r
  AC(sub,gi)@OMM+Gi(r,ac,act~1)@ML<->AC(sub,gi!1)@OMM.Gi(r,ac!1,act~1)@ML k8f,k8r

end reaction rules

end model
